# Home Dark Cards: project context

## Architecture and authority

- This repository contains local, vanilla JavaScript Home Assistant Lovelace custom
  elements under `home-dark-cards/`. It is not a standalone web application.
- There is no local package manifest, build system, application server, test server,
  database, or generated bundle. The browser runtime is the Home Assistant frontend.
- The local source and the live Home Assistant instance are separate authorities:
  source files describe card behavior; `user-hass` MCP reads and changes the live
  dashboard, resource registry, and entity state. Do not infer live dashboard
  structure from stale documentation when MCP can verify it.
- Do not edit Home Assistant `.storage` files. Do not modify remote configuration,
  commit, or push unless the user explicitly requests it.

## Deployment workflow

- Manually copy URL-based JavaScript files to
  `/config/www/home-dark-cards/` on the Home Assistant host.
- Register each URL-based file as an existing `module` resource at
  `/local/home-dark-cards/<file>.js`. MCP resource registration cannot upload the
  file to `/config/www`.
- Update an existing resource ID in place and use a cache-busting query suffix on
  the URL; never create duplicate resources for a changed card. Query suffixes are
  expected to change between deployments.
- After a deployment, verify `ha_config_list_dashboard_resources` and the
  `home-dark` dashboard config. Save dashboard config only when its card list
  changed, then hard-refresh the browser or force-close/reopen the Companion app.

## Verified live `home-dark` structure

- Dashboard URL path: `home-dark`; title: `Home Dashboard Dark`; storage mode.
- Views are native `sections` views: `home` (Home), `lights` (Lights), `climate`
  (Climate), `blinds` (Blinds), `media` (Media), and `vacuum` (Vacuum). Navigation
  is the native view strip, not a JavaScript screen state machine.
- The Home view contains the header, house status, Andrei and Loredana person
  cards, ten room tiles, door security, Tesla-style energy flow, and Roborock/
  Storm Trooper quick rows. Its section background is `#1a2433`.
- The ten curated rooms and popup hashes are: Living Room `#living-room`,
  Cinema `#cinema`, Office `#office`, Eric's Room `#erics-room`,
  Master Bedroom `#master-bedroom`, Kitchen `#kitchen`, Master Bathroom
  `#master-bathroom`, Eric's Bathroom `#erics-bathroom`, Studio Bathroom
  `#studio-bathroom`, and Garage `#garage`.
- Each room tile may navigate by setting `window.location.hash`; the matching
  `custom:bubble-card` with `card_type: pop-up` owns the popup. Popup names,
  hashes, backgrounds, `close_on_click`, headings, and child-card composition
  are dashboard configuration, not hardcoded in JavaScript.
- Dedicated live views group the curated controls by room: Climate has eight
  climate cards, Blinds has row controls plus cover cards, Media has Living
  Room/Cinema/Office media rows, and Vacuum has the `vacuum.roborock` row.
- Verified Home-view energy mappings are `sensor.whole_house_grid_power` for
  grid power, `sensor.shelly_pro3em_general_total_active_power` for load,
  `sensor.solix_c1000_x_state_of_charge` for battery level,
  `sensor.tesla_wall_connector_total_power` for EV power,
  `sensor.storm_trooper_battery_level` for EV battery,
  `switch.storm_trooper_charge` for charging, `device_tracker.storm_trooper_location`
  for EV presence, and `weather.openweathermap`/`sun.sun` for environment.

